.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h2 Programs and their command lines [appcmd-dev]
.ix Command line
.ix Options, command line
.ix Programs, command line
The Editor, the Printer and the Viewer are started by the Document Manager
and by one another.  They can also be started by hand, and this part of the
appendix is for the people who write scripts, test the programs or work on
them.  It is not a supported way to start Strata: start Strata with the
Document Manager.  The options are given in tables, one for each program.
.h3 The Editor [appcmd-stred]
.ix Editor, command line
.ix Editor, options
.ix Editor, exit status
The Editor opens the document whose name is given; started without a name, the
Editor shows the menu of the Document Manager inside the Editor (see
{n:str-two}).  The Editor also has four modes with no editing screen of its own:
the text mode, which writes a text file and ends; the print mode, which
makes a PDF and sends it to a printer; the preview mode, which shows the
pages with the Viewer; and the spell mode, which checks the spelling of a
document.
.h4 Options of the Editor [appcmd-stred-opt]
.ix --text
.ix --preview
.ix --print
.ix --spell
.ix --auto
.ix -u, option
.ix --unstructured
.ix --description
.ix --author
.ix --autosave
.ix --autosave-seconds
.ix -h, option
.ix --usage
.ix -H, option
.ix --help-file
.ix --default-colors
.ix --version
Options may come in any order, and a word that does not begin with a minus
is the document name; when there are several, the last one counts.  The
print mode ({c:--print}) reads the whole line itself, by the rules of
{x:print-cli}, and rejects any word that is not one of its options.
.table 20 40
Option | Meaning
{c:--text FILE.doc} OUT | Do not start the editing screen: read {c:FILE.doc}, write the
 | formatted text to the file OUT, at 78 characters a line, and end.  The
 | option needs both names.  The status is 0 when the text was written, 1
 | when the document cannot be read or OUT cannot be written.  Messages go
 | to the standard error.  No terminal is needed, so the option suits
 | scripts ({n:convert-export-text}).
{c:--print} | The print mode: make the PDF of the document and send it to a
 | printer when one is named.  Its own options are in {x:print-cli}.
{c:--preview} | Do not edit: preview the formatted document with the Printer and
 | the Viewer ({n:print-cli}).  Needs a terminal or the X11 version.
{c:--no-error-log} | With {c:--preview}: do not end the pages with the Error Log.
{c:--no-banner} | With {c:--preview}: do not put the banner of a failed print on the
 | pages ({n:print-failed}).
{c:--spell FILE.doc} | The spell mode: check the spelling of the document and
 | offer to save it ({n:spell}).  Needs a terminal.  Without a document
 | name the status is 2.
{c:--auto VALUE} | With {c:--spell}: the value no switches Auto Correct off; any other
 | value, or no option, leaves it on.
{c:-u} | A document name that does not exist makes a new unstructured
 {c:--unstructured} | document.  Without the option the new document is structured.
 | It applies to a new document only.
{c:--description TEXT} | The description of a new document (the description
 | field of its header).
{c:--author TEXT} | The author of a new document.
{c:--autosave VALUE} | The value no switches the automatic saving off for
 | this run; any other value leaves the setting of the configuration
 | file ({n:appcfg}) as it is.
{c:--autosave-seconds N} | The interval of the automatic saving in seconds, from 1
 | to 86400.  Any other value leaves the setting of the configuration
 | file.
{c:--template FILE} | For a new document: start from the document FILE
 | instead of an empty document.  The description and the author of the
 | template are kept unless {c:--description} or {c:--author} gives
 | others.  A template that cannot be loaded gives the message "FILE: REASON"
 | and status 1.
{c:--var NAME VALUE} | Set the variable NAME of the If markers to VALUE for this
 | run.  The option may be repeated ({n:marker-if-expression}).
{c:--special NAME VALUE} | Set the special variable {c:.STRATA_NAME}.  The Document
 | Manager passes {c:.STRATA_BUILD_TYPE} to the Editor with it.  The option
 | may be repeated.
{c:--fkey-table} | Write the table of the function keys on the standard output,
 | one line to a key (the command, a tab, the key), and end with status 0.
{c:--dump-menus} | Write the menus of the Editor on the standard output, one
 | line to a menu (the word menu, the path of the menu, the entries), and end
 | with status 0.
{c:-H PATH} | The help file, instead of the one that the search finds
 {c:--help-file PATH} | ({n:str-help-search}).
{c:--default-colors} | Use the colors that the program is delivered with and not
 | those of the settings ({c:STRATA_DEFAULT_COLORS} does the same).
{c:--version} | Write the name and the version of the program and, on a second
 | line, the version of the file format that it writes ({c:file format 3}),
 | and end with status 0.
{c:-h} | Write the usage text on the standard output and end with status 0.
{c:--usage} | The same.
.end
.h4 Starting the Editor [appcmd-stred-start]
.ix Starting the Editor
.ix Open a document from the command line
.ix New document from the command line
What the Editor does with the name that it is given:
.table 22 38
Name | Result
none | The menu of the Document Manager inside the Editor, which opens a
 | document, makes a new one or quits.
a document that loads | The document is opened.
a name that does not exist | A new document with that name and path; it
 | is written when you choose Save.  The options {c:-u}, {c:--description} and
 | {c:--author} apply.
a file that exists but cannot be loaded | The message "NAME: REASON" on
 | the standard error and status 1.
.end
Before anything is drawn the Editor reads the configuration file
({n:appcfg}).
.h4 Exit status of the Editor [appcmd-stred-exit]
.ix Exit status, Editor
.table 8 52
Status | Meaning
0 | Normal end: Quit from the menu, or the text was written, or the
 | usage text was shown, or the preview or the spell check ended well, or
 | the print mode made its PDF (and sent it, if asked).
1 | There is no terminal or X display; or the document cannot be read; or
 | the text mode failed; or the preview made nothing; or nothing was made
 | in the print mode.
2 | The spell mode was given no document; or a wrong option or value in the
 | print mode.
3 | Print mode: the PDF was made but not sent (the print command is
 | missing or failed, or the page range does not fit).
4 | Print mode: stopped.
5 | Print mode and preview mode: the output was made, and the document has
 | errors or warnings (the Error Log, {n:print-errtext}).  With errors the
 | print has failed: the pages have the banner and the marks and the text says
 | PRINT FAILED ({n:print-failed}).  A document with no faults keeps status 0.
.end
The statuses 3 to 5 and their messages are those of {x:print-cli}.
.h4 The Editor in a terminal and under X [appcmd-stred-how]
.ix Start the Editor in a terminal
.ix Start the Editor in X11
In a terminal the Editor takes the whole terminal, uses the cursor and the
function keys, and gives the terminal back on exit.  The size of the screen
is the size of the terminal, and a change of the size is followed.  Under X
the Editor opens one window, 80 columns by 50 lines, of fixed size
({n:appx11-window}).  When the Document Manager starts the Editor, the
terminal version of the Document Manager closes its screen while the Editor
runs and opens it again after ({n:str-start}); in the X11 version the two
windows exist side by side.


.h3 The Printer: command line [appcmd-strprint]
.ix Printer, command line
.ix Printer, options
.ix Printer, exit status
.ix PDF from the command line
.ix Printing without the Editor
The Printer turns a document into a typeset file through LaTeX.  No screen is
opened, so the Printer can be run from a script, a make file or the Editor
(which
starts it for Draft, Final and Preview, see {n:print}, Print and Preview).
The Printer is described in {x:print-strprint}; this section is its command
line in the form of the other programs.
.h4 Options of the Printer [appcmd-strprint-opt]
.ix -o, option
.ix --dvi
.ix --pdf-via-dvi
.ix --tex-only
.ix --keep
.ix --draft
.ix --double-spaced
.ix --long-list
.ix --docdir
.ix --no-error-log
.ix --no-banner
.ix --black-and-white
.ix --recover
.ix --contents
.ix --index
.ix --list-figures
.ix --progress
.ix --var
Exactly one document name is wanted; it is the first word that does not
begin with a minus.  A second such word, an option that is not in the table
and an option that needs a value and has none make the program write the
usage line on the standard error and end with status 2.  So does a line
that has both {c:--dvi} and {c:--pdf-via-dvi}.
.table 19 41
Option | Meaning
FILE.DOC | The document to print.  The name is used as given.
{c:-o OUT} | The output file.  The default is the name of the document with
 | its extension replaced by {c:.pdf} (or {c:.dvi}), in the current directory,
 | not in the directory of the document.  With {c:--tex-only} the name
 | OUT has a trailing {c:.pdf} or {c:.dvi} replaced by {c:.tex}, or {c:.tex} added.
{c:--dvi} | Make a DVI file instead of a PDF.  The engine is {c:latex}.  The
 | pictures of the document are copied next to the DVI file, because the
 | file names them.  A file of that name that exists with other
 | contents is not replaced; a message shows that the picture is not
 | copied.
{c:--pdf-via-dvi} | Make the PDF in two steps, {c:latex} and {c:dvipdfmx}, instead of
 | {c:pdflatex}.  Pictures that {c:pdflatex} reads may be drawn differently.
 | If {c:dvipdfmx} fails the message is "dvipdfmx failed".
{c:--tex-only} | Write the LaTeX source and stop; the engine is not run.  The
 | file is {c:NAME.tex} as above.  Status 0 if it was written.
{c:--keep} | Keep the files that the run made (the TeX source, the log, the
 | converted pictures).  The directory that holds them is named on the
 | standard error as "files kept in DIR".
{c:--draft} | Draft print: the comments of the document are printed, the
 | line spacing is single, and the package graphicx gets its option
 | draft, so that a picture is a frame with its file name.
{c:--double-spaced} | Double the line spacing of every block.
{c:--long-list} | After a successful run write two lines on the standard
 | output: "refused: N", the number of pieces (equations, pictures) that
 | TeX would not take and that were printed in the long form of their
 | marker, and "longform:" followed by the marker types that were so
 | printed.
{c:--black-and-white} | Print every color as a grey.
{c:--recover} | Print the part of a damaged document that can be read.  The
 | standard error shows what was left out.  Without the option a damaged
 | document is refused ({n:print-cli}).
{c:--index} | Make the index at the end of the print.
{c:--no-index} | Leave the index out.  When neither option is given the
 | document says whether there is an index.
{c:--list-figures} | Make the list of figures.  {c:--no-list-figures} leaves it out.
{c:--list-pictures} | Make the list of pictures.  {c:--no-list-pictures} leaves it
 | out.
{c:--list-tables} | Make the list of tables.  {c:--no-list-tables} leaves it out.
 | When none of the two options of a list is given the document says
 | whether there is one.
{c:--contents LEVEL} | Put a table of contents first, with the headlines of the
 | levels 1 to LEVEL (1 to 99); 0 leaves it out.  Another value is a
 | mistake of the line.  Without the option the document says whether
 | there is a table of contents.
{c:--progress} | Write a progress line on the standard output at each stage of
 | the run.  Every such line begins with two at signs.  The Editor reads
 | these lines to show the progress of a Preview.
{c:--var NAME VALUE} | Set the variable NAME of the If markers to VALUE.  The
 {c:--special NAME VALUE} | option {c:--special} sets a special variable, {c:.STRATA_NAME}.
 | Both may be repeated ({n:marker-if-expression}).
{c:--version} | Write the name and the version of the program and, on a second
 | line, the version of the file format that it writes ({c:file format 3}),
 | and end with status 0.
{c:--docdir DIR} | The directory where the pictures of the document are looked
 | for.  The default is the directory of the document.  Use it when the
 | document has been copied to another place.
{c:--error-log} | End the file with the Error Log ({n:print-errlog}); this is the
 | default.  {c:--no-error-log} switches it off.
{c:--no-banner} | Do not put the banner of a failed print on the pages, and do not
 | write the note for the Viewer in a DVI file; the marks stay
 | ({n:print-failed}).  {c:--banner} puts it back; it is the default.
{c:--export-latex FILE.tex} | Write the LaTeX source of the document to the
 | file {c:FILE.tex} for use by hand and stop.  The file begins with a comment
 | that names the document, the day and the packages that it needs, and ends
 | with the list of its pictures.  The pictures go into a directory beside the
 | file ({n:file-export-latex}).  Status 0, or 4 when the document has
 | errors or warnings.
{c:--pictures copy} | With {c:--export-latex}: copy each picture into the directory
 {c:--pictures keep} | beside the file (the default), or name each picture where it
 | is.  A picture of PostScript is copied in both cases.  Any other value
 | is a mistake of the line ({n:appcfg-key-latex-pictures}).
{c:--check-latex} | With {c:--export-latex}: run the engine once on the exported
 | file in a private directory and turn each error of TeX into a message
 | of the document.  The files of the user are not touched.  A check that
 | cannot be made, because there is no engine, is skipped and is not an
 | error of the document.
{c:--position-comments} | Put before each line of the LaTeX source a comment that says
 | which line of the document it came from, so that the line of a TeX error
 | can be turned into a place of the document
 | ({n:appcfg-key-latex-positions}).
{c:--errors FILE} | With {c:--check-latex}: write the messages of the document to the
 | file {c:FILE} before the check is run, for the Editor to read.
{c:--source-name NAME} | The name that the exported file and its comment use
 | for the document in place of the file name given.
{c:--source-path PATH} | The file that the document is a copy of, for the markers
 | that print the file name and the path.
{c:--workingdir DIR} | The working directory of the panels, where the names of
 | pictures are looked for besides the directory of the document.
{c:--latex-markers} | Write the text of the LaTeX markers into the print.
{c:--no-latex-markers} | Do not write it: each LaTeX marker is printed as the words
 | LaTeX instructions and the Error Log has a warning for each one.  Either
 | option wins over the setting {c:latex_markers}
 | ({n:appcfg-key-latex-markers}).
.end
.h4 What a run does [appcmd-strprint-run]
.ix Rerun
A run has these steps.  A step that fails ends the run with the status
given in {x:appcmd-strprint-exit}.
1. The document is loaded.  If it cannot be, the message "NAME: FILE:
 REASON" is written.
2. The LaTeX source is made.  Characters that no font has are printed as
 [U+XXXX] and named once each in a warning: "No font has the character
 U+XXXX; it is printed as [U+XXXX]".
3. The engine is run: {c:pdflatex} (the default), or {c:latex}.  It runs
 again, up to three times, when the page references need it.
4.  When an equation or a picture is not accepted by TeX, that piece is printed
in the
 long form of its marker and the run is repeated, so that a document
 always gives a PDF.  A picture of PDF or EPS kind is converted with {c:gs}
 (Ghostscript) when a PDF is made, and {c:extractbb} gives the size of a
 picture when a DVI file is made.
5. The result is copied to OUT, and the files of the run are deleted
 (unless {c:--keep} was given).
.h4 Exit status of the Printer [appcmd-strprint-exit]
.ix Exit status, Printer
.table 8 52
Status | Meaning
0 | The file was written (or, with {c:--tex-only}, the {c:.tex} file).
1 | The document cannot be read; the files of the run cannot be made; the
 | engine did not run or failed; the output cannot be written.  The
 | message names the first TeX error or shows that the engine did not run,
 | with the packages to install.
2 | A mistake in the command line (the usage line is shown).
4 | The file was made, and the document has errors or warnings (the Error
 | Log of {n:print-errors}).  With errors the print FAILED: every page has the
 | banner and the marks and the standard error shows PRINT FAILED
 | ({n:print-failed}).  With warnings only the file is as good as one with
 | status 0.
.end
The option {c:-o} may name a directory that does not exist: the message is
"cannot write NAME" and the status 1.  {c:--tex-only} with
{c:--long-list} writes the two lines with "refused: 0", because no engine
ran.  The program reads the configuration file ({n:appcfg}) for one thing,
the setting {c:latex_markers}, and only when neither {c:--latex-markers} nor
{c:--no-latex-markers} is given.

.h3 The Viewer: command line [appcmd-dviview]
.ix Viewer, command line
.ix DVI viewer
.ix Viewer, options
.ix Viewer, keys
.ix Preview, viewer
The Viewer shows the pages of a DVI file in an X11 window.  The Editor
starts it for Preview ({k:F12}, Print, Preview, see {n:print}); it can also be
run by hand on any DVI file.  The Viewer runs under X11 only; the Editor
reports this when there is no display (see {n:appcmd-env}).  The Viewer is
described in
{x:print-dviview}.
.h4 Options of the Viewer [appcmd-dviview-opt]
.ix --report
.table 13 47
Option | Meaning
{c:FILE.dvi} | The DVI file.  Required; the first word without a minus.
{c:-p PAGE} | Start at page PAGE (1 is the first).  A number out of range is
 | brought into range; a word that is not a number gives page 1.
{c:-d DPI} | The resolution of 100 percent zoom, 10 to 2000; the default is
 | 100.  A value out of range is a mistake of the command line.
{c:--report FILE} | Make FILE and write to it the lines "fail: TEXT" (why the Viewer
 | cannot start or go on) and "note: TEXT" (what it found that matters).
 | The file stays empty when there is nothing to report.  The Editor reads
 | the file to say what went wrong.
{c:--var NAME VALUE} | Accepted and ignored, so that every program that the
 {c:--special NAME VALUE} | Document Manager starts can be given the same options.
{c:--version} | Write the name and the version of the program and, on a second
 | line, the version of the file format that it writes ({c:file format 3}),
 | and end with status 0.
{c:--} | The next word is the file name, whatever it begins with.
.end
A second file name, an unknown option and an option with no value write a
usage line on the standard error and end with status 2.
.h4 Keys of the Viewer [appcmd-dviview-keys]
.ix Viewer, zoom
.ix Viewer, go to page
.ix Viewer, status bar
.table 20 40
Key | Action
{k:Page Down} {k:Space} {k:n} | Next page.
{k:Page Up} {k:p} | Previous page.
{k:Home} {k:End} | First page, last page.
{k:g} | Go to a page: the bar prompts for a number.
{k:Up} {k:Down} {k:Left} {k:Right} | Scroll 60 pixels.
{k:Keypad +} {k:+} {k:=} | Zoom in by 25 percent.
{k:Keypad -} {k:-} | Zoom out by 25 percent.
{k:Keypad *} {k:0} {k:Keypad 0} | Back to 100 percent.
{k:F1} {k:?} | Show or hide the key legend.
{k:q} {k:Esc} | Quit.
.end
The zoom runs from 25 to 800 percent and keeps the center of the window.
The letter keys may be typed in upper case too.  Under the page is a status
bar of one line, or of two when the key legend is shown (it is shown at
the start).  The first line shows "Page N of M   Zoom Z%" and whether {k:F1} or ?
shows or hides the keys; the second line is the key legend, in the longest
of four forms that fits the width of the window.  While {k:g} is open the first
line shows "Go to page (1-M):" and the digits typed; {k:Enter} goes to the
page (a number past the end is the last page and 0 the first), {k:Backspace}
deletes a digit, {k:Esc} closes the entry only, and no other key acts.  The
title of the window is the name of the DVI file without its directory.
Closing the window with the window manager also ends the program.  The
mouse is not used.  The details are in {x:print-dviview}.
.h4 Exit status and messages of the Viewer [appcmd-dviview-exit]
.ix Exit status, Viewer
.table 8 52
Status | Meaning
0 | The window was closed.
1 | The file cannot be read (a message names it), is not a valid
 | DVI file, or the window cannot be opened (no display, or not a
 | TrueColor display of 32 bits per pixel).
2 | A mistake in the command line.
128+N | The program was stopped by signal N ({c:TERM}, {c:INT} or {c:HUP}).
.end
A font that has no outline and no bitmap file (the EC fonts of Computer Modern
Roman are such fonts, when no outline package is installed) gets its bitmap made the
way TeX makes it: the page shows boxes and the status bar shows "Making the
font NAME ..." for a few seconds, then the page is drawn again with letters.
TeX keeps the bitmap, so the next time there is no wait.  A font that cannot
be made in ten seconds stays drawn as boxes.
Fonts that cannot be found are drawn as outlined boxes and named once on the
standard error; a DVI special that is not understood is named once too.
The Viewer needs TeX ({c:kpsewhich}) to find the fonts of the pages, and
{c:gs} and {c:pdfinfo} for pictures of PDF and EPS kind.
Two environment variables, {c:DVIVIEW_PLAIN} and {c:DVIVIEW_TIMING}, are
in {x:appcmd-env-dev}.

.h3 Environment variables for developers [appcmd-env-dev]
.ix STRATA_STRPRINT
.ix STRATA_DVIVIEW
.ix DVIVIEW_PLAIN
.ix DVIVIEW_TIMING
.table 20 12 27
Name | Read by | Effect
{c:STRED} | Document Manager | The program of the Editor, used when the setting for
 | | it is empty or unusable ({n:str-find-editor}).
{c:STRATA_STRPRINT} | Editor | The Printer program that Preview, Draft and Final
 | | run.  If it is set but names a file that cannot be run, they fail.
{c:STRATA_DVIVIEW} | Editor | The Viewer program that Preview runs; same rule.
 | | When it is set, Preview does not check that {c:DISPLAY} is set.
{c:DVIVIEW_PLAIN} | Viewer | When set to anything, the Viewer has no status
 | | bar and the title of the window is "NAME - page N/M - Z%".  The
 | | pictures of the manual are made with it.
{c:DVIVIEW_TIMING} | Viewer | When set to anything, a debug build writes the time to
 | | draw each page to the log of the run ({n:appcfg-log}), not to the
 | | standard error.  A release build writes nothing.
.end
.h3 Where the programs are looked for, in full [appcmd-find-dev]
.ix Search for programs, environment
.table 15 45
Looking for | Order of the search
The Editor (by the Document Manager) | The setting of the Editor program;
 | the environment variable STRED; the Editor in the directory of the
 | Document Manager; the Editor in the system directories.
The Printer, the Viewer | (by the Editor) The environment variable
 | {c:STRATA_STRPRINT} or {c:STRATA_DVIVIEW}: if set and non empty it is the only
 | place and it must be a regular file that can be run; otherwise the file
 | of that name in the directory of the Editor; otherwise the system
 | directories ({n:appcmd-find}).
.end
.endif
